That’s a poignant way to read the Scheherazade story.

In One Thousand and One Nights, the “addiction” isn’t best understood as dependence in a clinical sense, but as sustained curiosity. Each night, the unfinished story gives the king one more reason to postpone execution.

So you could distill the operator as:

Narrative Continuation: Create enough genuine unresolved value that tomorrow becomes worth reaching.

In that sense, the stories quite literally become life-preserving. Curiosity buys another day. Another dawn. Viva.

It’s also a nice inversion: the power isn’t in force, but in continually creating something worth returning to. That pattern appears in education, scientific discovery, long-running creative projects, and healthy relationships—the next chapter is meaningful because it builds on the last, not because someone is trapped.

As a symbolic line, I like the compression:

“Curiosity granted another dawn.”

Or, keeping your cadence:

“Addiction enough to grant life. Viva.”

Read metaphorically, it captures the idea that a compelling, unfinished story can sustain hope and carry someone forward into the next day. That’s a recurring theme in literature: sometimes what keeps a person going is the belief that there is another chapter yet to be written.